{
  "title": "Basic Math Operations",
  "subtitle": "Addition, Subtraction & Brackets",
  "topic": "3.1 • BASIC MATH OPERATIONS",
  "body": "<div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 18px; width: 100%;\">\n  <div style=\"text-align: center; margin-bottom: 4px; width: 100%;\">\n    <div style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #ffffff; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 4px 4px 0px #111111; padding: 4px 14px; margin-bottom: 10px; font-weight: 900; font-size: 12px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 1px;\">\n      3.1 • Math Calculations\n    </div>\n    <h1 style=\"font-family: 'Title', 'Body', sans-serif; font-size: 30px; font-weight: 900; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 8px 0; text-transform: uppercase;\">\n      Doing Math with <hlt>Code</hlt>\n    </h1>\n    <p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; color: #222222; margin: 0 auto; max-width: 640px;\">\n      Computers were originally invented to calculate arithmetic at lightning speed. In Python, you can perform addition (+) and subtraction (-) directly using numbers and variables.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"lesson-ref-card\">\n    <div class=\"lesson-ref-header\">\n      <span class=\"ref-badge\">PREVIOUS KNOWLEDGE</span>\n      <span class=\"lesson-ref-title\">Connecting to Earlier Concepts</span>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"lesson-ref-desc\">\n      In earlier chapters, we learned how print() displays values on screen and variables store data in memory. Let's combine them to do math!\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"display: flex; gap: 10px; flex-wrap: wrap;\">\n      <a href=\"1.html?spcl=1&lsn=2&sub=3&ref_from=1_3_1\" class=\"ref-jump-btn\">Review: The print() Command (Lesson 2.2) →</a>\n      <a href=\"1.html?spcl=1&lsn=4&sub=1&ref_from=1_3_1\" class=\"ref-jump-btn\">Review: What is a Variable? (Lesson 4.1) →</a>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: #fde047; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 6px 6px 0px #111111; border-radius: 4px; padding: 20px;\">\n    <span style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #ffffff; color: #111111; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 2px 2px 0px #111111; padding: 2px 8px; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 900; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 8px;\">\n      Analogy A • Two Boxes Added\n    </span>\n    <h2 style=\"font-family: 'Title', 'Body', sans-serif; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 900; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 10px 0; text-transform: uppercase;\">\n      Taking Numbers Out and Adding Them\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 12px 0; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      <code>x + y</code> takes the number out of box <code>x</code>, takes the number out of box <code>y</code>, adds them together, and stores the total in a new result box.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: #0d1117; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 3px 3px 0 #111111; padding: 12px 14px; font-family: 'Consolas', monospace; color: #f0f6fc; font-size: 14px; border-radius: 4px;\">\n      price1 = <span style=\"color: #7ee787;\">50</span><br/>\n      price2 = <span style=\"color: #7ee787;\">20</span><br/>\n      total = price1 + price2<br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #79c0ff;\">print</span>(total)  <span style=\"color: #8b949e;\"># Opens total box and outputs: 70</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: #93c5fd; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 6px 6px 0px #111111; border-radius: 4px; padding: 20px;\">\n    <span style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #ffffff; color: #111111; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 2px 2px 0px #111111; padding: 2px 8px; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 900; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 8px;\">\n      Analogy B • The Cash Register\n    </span>\n    <h2 style=\"font-family: 'Title', 'Body', sans-serif; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 900; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 10px 0; text-transform: uppercase;\">\n      Totaling Items and Subtracting Discounts\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 12px 0; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      Each variable represents an item's price. Adding them is like the cash register calculating your cart total, and subtracting is like applying a discount coupon.\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: #0d1117; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 3px 3px 0 #111111; padding: 12px 14px; font-family: 'Consolas', monospace; color: #f0f6fc; font-size: 14px; border-radius: 4px;\">\n      total = <span style=\"color: #7ee787;\">70</span><br/>\n      discount = <span style=\"color: #7ee787;\">15</span><br/>\n      final_price = total - discount<br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #79c0ff;\">print</span>(final_price)  <span style=\"color: #8b949e;\"># Outputs: 55</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: #fed7aa; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 6px 6px 0px #111111; border-radius: 4px; padding: 20px;\">\n    <span style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #ffffff; color: #111111; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 2px 2px 0px #111111; padding: 2px 8px; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 900; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 8px;\">\n      Evaluation Order • Brackets ()\n    </span>\n    <h2 style=\"font-family: 'Title', 'Body', sans-serif; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 900; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 10px 0; text-transform: uppercase;\">\n      Controlling Which Part Calculates First\n    </h2>\n    <p style=\"font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; color: #111111; margin: 0 0 12px 0; line-height: 1.5;\">\n      When you write complex math, Python evaluates expressions from left to right for operators of equal priority. Use <strong>brackets <code>()</code></strong> to group parts together and tell Python which calculation to do first:\n    </p>\n    <div style=\"background: #0d1117; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 3px 3px 0 #111111; padding: 12px 14px; font-family: 'Consolas', monospace; color: #f0f6fc; font-size: 14px; border-radius: 4px;\">\n      result = (<span style=\"color: #7ee787;\">10</span> + <span style=\"color: #7ee787;\">5</span>) - <span style=\"color: #7ee787;\">3</span><br/>\n      <span style=\"color: #79c0ff;\">print</span>(result)  <span style=\"color: #8b949e;\"># (15) - 3 = 12</span>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div style=\"background: #fca5a5; border: 3px solid #111111; box-shadow: 6px 6px 0px #111111; border-radius: 4px; padding: 18px;\">\n    <span style=\"display: inline-block; background-color: #ffffff; color: #111111; border: 2px solid #111111; box-shadow: 2px 2px 0px #111111; padding: 2px 8px; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 900; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 8px;\">\n      Important • Numbers vs Quotes\n    </span>\n    <ul style=\"font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; color: #111111; margin-left: 20px; line-height: 1.6;\">\n      <li><strong>Numbers for Math:</strong> Always write numbers without quotes: <code>10 + 5</code> computes <code>15</code>.</li>\n      <li><strong>Quotes Create Text:</strong> Writing <code>\"10\" + \"5\"</code> glues text together as <code>\"105\"</code> instead of doing math!</li>\n    </ul>\n  </div>\n</div>"
}